@node AI assistant
@chapter AI assistant

@cindex AI
@cindex assistant
@cindex Ollama
@cindex Claude
@cindex agent
@cindex chat
@cindex large language model

Where a language server (@pxref{Language servers}) @emph{understands} your code,
the @dfn{AI assistant} lets you @emph{talk} to a large language model from
inside the editor: ask a question, have it rewrite the open file (or just a
marked block), edit several files together, or run as an autonomous agent -- all
in the bottom pane, next to compiler and debugger output, driven from the
keyboard like everything else.

@subheading Which mode do I use?

@table @asis
@item @kbd{Alt-G a} Ask
A read-only conversation.  Best for ``what does this do?'', ``how would I@dots{}?''
-- it never changes a file.
@item @kbd{Alt-G e} Edit
Rewrite @emph{one} file (or a @emph{marked block}) from an instruction, reviewed
as a diff.  Best for a focused change in the file you are in.
@item @kbd{Alt-G f} Multi-file edit
Study the workspace, then propose a coordinated change across @emph{several}
files, approved together.  Best for a change that touches more than one file.
(Some tools call this a ``workspace edit''; note it is @emph{not} the ``plan
mode'' of an agent, which merely drafts steps.)
@item @kbd{Alt-G g} Agent
An autonomous loop that reads, greps, writes files and runs commands under a
permission dial, step by step.  Best for an open-ended task (``add a test for
@code{foo} and make it pass'').
@item @kbd{Alt-G b} Build & fix (agent)
A canned agent that builds, reads the errors, edits, and rebuilds until it
passes.
@end table

In short: @strong{Ask} answers, @strong{Edit} changes one file, @strong{Multi-file
edit} changes several at once, and @strong{Agent} takes actions on its own.

The assistant is @strong{off unless you ask for it}, twice over.  It is compiled
only when xwpe is configured with @option{--enable-ai}, so a normal build
contains none of it and is byte-for-byte the classic editor.  Even in a build
that has it, it does nothing until you turn it on.  This is deliberate: the
appeal is @dfn{the best old interface, optionally superpowered}, never
@dfn{another AI editor that happens to look retro}.

It is also @strong{local-first}.  The default backend is Ollama
(@uref{https://ollama.com}) running on your own machine -- no account, no API
key, no telemetry, and it works over SSH.  Cloud models are opt-in.

@section Enabling the assistant

@cindex AI, enabling
Configure and build with the feature on:

@example
./configure --enable-ai                    # local backends only
./configure --enable-ai --enable-ai-tls    # also HTTPS (Claude, remote OpenAI)
./configure --enable-ai --enable-ai-agent-host  # also host the Claude Code CLI
make
@end example

Every mode asks for its instruction in a wide prompt box that holds a long,
detailed request; @key{Enter} sends, @key{Esc} cancels.  For a real multi-line
prompt, press the @samp{Multi-line} button (@key{Alt-M}): it opens a scratch
editor window as a text area, so you compose with the full editor (several
lines, cut and paste, the arrow keys), finish with @key{Esc}, and confirm Send.

Then turn it on at run time in one of two ways: tick @samp{Enable AI assistant}
under @samp{Options}, @samp{AI}, or start with @env{XWPE_AI_ENABLE=1}.  Pressing
@key{Alt-G} while it is off writes a line in the @samp{AI} pane that names that
dialog.  With it on
a clickable @samp{Alt-G AI} entry appears on the bottom status bar and
@key{Alt-G} opens the assistant's action menu -- the same way @key{Alt-F} opens
the @samp{File} menu.  Pick an entry with the arrow keys and @key{Enter}, or
press its letter; the letter also works as type-ahead, so @kbd{Alt-G a} jumps
straight to Ask (programming mode only, i.e.@: run as @command{wpe} or
@command{xwpe}):

@table @kbd
@item Alt-G a
Ask -- chat.
@item Alt-G e
Edit the current file (or a marked block).
@item Alt-G f
Multi-file edit -- change several files together.
@item Alt-G g
Agent -- an autonomous loop.
@item Alt-G b
Build & fix -- an agent that builds until it passes.
@item Alt-G y
cycle the permission level (ask @result{} edits @result{} auto).  It does not
open a window: it @dfn{flashes} a one-line confirmation across the bottom status
bar showing all three levels with the active one highlighted, so you see which
is selected and what it does; the next keystroke restores the key hints.  The
choice is remembered automatically across sessions.
@item Alt-G n
Clear conversation -- forget the workspace's history.  Also a status-bar flash,
not a window.
@item Alt-G s
AI settings -- open @samp{Options}, @samp{AI} (backend, model, permission level,
enable toggle).
@end table

The same actions are on the @samp{AI} menu, which groups the four modes and
@samp{Build & fix} above a divider, then the quick permission toggle,
@samp{Clear conversation} and @samp{AI settings@dots{}}.  The backend, model and
enable toggle live in @samp{Options}, @samp{AI}, reached with @kbd{Alt-G s}.

The very first time you invoke an action, a one-time notice states the trust
model (local by default, opt-in, every edit previewed and revertible with
@kbd{Ctrl-U}) and asks you to confirm with @key{Enter} or @kbd{y} (or decline
with @kbd{n} or @key{Esc}).  Answering it with an action letter counts as the
confirmation and runs that action, so a habitual @kbd{Alt-G a} still lands
straight in Ask on the first use.

@section Backends

@cindex AI, backends
The backend is chosen with @code{AIBackend} (or @env{XWPE_AI_BACKEND}); the same
chat / edit / multi-file / agent interface works with all of them.

@table @asis
@item @code{ollama} (default)
Ollama's HTTP API on @code{http://localhost:11434}.  Local, free, no key.  The
model list comes from the server; @kbd{Alt-G s} (@samp{Options}, @samp{AI})
picks one with @key{Alt-M}.
@item @code{openai}
Any OpenAI-compatible server -- @command{llama.cpp}, LM Studio, vLLM, Groq,
OpenRouter, OpenAI itself, or a local bridge to another provider.  Set its URL in
the @samp{Endpoint URL} field of @samp{Options}, @samp{AI} (@key{Alt-U}), or with
@code{AIEndpoint}; an API key comes from @env{OPENAI_API_KEY}.  The request path
is taken relative to the URL you give, so the endpoint may carry the version
segment the provider documents -- @code{https://api.groq.com/openai/v1},
@code{https://openrouter.ai/api/v1}, @code{https://api.openai.com/v1} -- while a
bare host (@code{http://localhost:11434}) defaults to @code{/v1}.  The endpoint
defaults to the local Ollama port, which also answers @code{/v1/models}, so until
you point it at your own server the model list (and the picker's title) shows
that Ollama endpoint.
@item @code{claude}
Anthropic's Messages API.  Needs @option{--enable-ai-tls} and
@env{ANTHROPIC_API_KEY}.
@item @code{claudecli}
Runs the @command{claude} command-line tool (Claude Code) as a subprocess, using
@emph{its} login.  No API key, no extra dependency, and it draws on your Claude
subscription rather than per-token API billing.  Offered only when
@command{claude} is on @env{PATH}.
@item @code{mock}
A deterministic in-process backend used by the test suite; no network.
@end table

@section Chat

@cindex AI, chat
@kbd{Alt-G a} asks for a prompt and streams the reply into the @samp{AI} pane.
The file the chat was opened from (and the selection marked in it, if any) is
sent as context, so ``what does this function do?'' works; the pane's own
transcript is never sent as the file, and when that file window has been
closed no file goes with the question.  Chat can also @emph{investigate the workspace}: it
is given the list of workspace files and may read any of them (or grep, or list
a directory) on its own before answering, so ``what does @file{config.h}
define?'' works even when that file is not open.  The editor stays fully
interactive while the reply streams.  The conversation is remembered for the
workspace (see @emph{Sessions}, below).

@cindex AI, pane placement
The pane docks along the bottom of the screen like the @samp{Messages}
window, under the file windows, and takes the bottom half; the file windows
are shortened to end above it, never covered.  When the @samp{Messages}
window is open as well (a build with @kbd{F9}, a changeset review) the two
share the bottom strip from the same row, @samp{Messages} on the left half
and the pane on the right, whichever of them opened first.  Window > Tile
and Window > Cascade arrange the file windows above the strip and leave the
pane where it is, as they leave @samp{Messages}.

The pane opens with a header line, @samp{[AI: <backend> | model: <model> |
policy: <dial>]}, and two hint lines naming the input keys and the slash
shortcuts; they appear the first time the chat is opened in a session, even
when another action had already written to the pane.  When the backend, the
model or the permission dial change while the chat is open (Options > AI
closed with Ok, @kbd{Alt-G y}, a provider switch), a new header line with
the current values precedes the next turn; the transcript above it is kept.

@cindex AI, pane colours
The pane tells who wrote a line by its colour, taken from the colour scheme,
with the prefix as the second cue.  In the default scheme your own lines
(@samp{You: @dots{}}) are white on the pane's blue; the assistant's reply is in
the editor text colour; the machinery lines (@samp{[agent] @dots{}},
@samp{[AI edit] @dots{}}, the header, the checkpoint line) are light grey, the
colour of a comment; a line that waits for a key (@samp{>> @dots{}}) is white
on green, like a dialog's default button; an error (@samp{AI: error: @dots{}},
a transport or backend error, @samp{[AI unavailable]}) is light red.  The four
entries are the @samp{AI pane Colors} section of @samp{Options}, @samp{Adjust
Colors} (@samp{User}, @samp{Machinery}, @samp{Attention}, @samp{Error}) and the
keys @code{aiu}, @code{aim}, @code{aia} and @code{aie} of the colour section
of @file{xwperc}; they are saved and restored with the scheme.  On a terminal
without colour your lines are bold, the machinery dim, the attention line in
reverse video and an error bold.

While the model works on a question the pane shows @samp{AI: (gathering the
answer...)} with the same rotating indicator, seconds count and @samp{(Esc
cancels)} as the other actions.  A chat turn is busy like any other: @key{Esc}
while the reply streams cancels the turn, the pane says @samp{[AI] cancelled}
and the input row is ready again; @key{Esc} with no turn running leaves the
pane and returns to the file window.  A second question sent while a reply
streams is not lost and does not cut the running turn: it is queued, the pane
says so, and it starts by itself when the running turn ends.

@cindex AI, slash commands
A leading @dfn{slash command} expands to a full, file-scoped instruction, so a
common ask is one token: @code{/explain}, @code{/fix}, @code{/test}, @code{/doc},
@code{/review}.  Text after the command is kept as a specific focus
(@code{/fix the off-by-one}); the transcript shows what you typed and only the
model receives the expansion.

Chat streams token by token.  @emph{Edit}, @emph{Multi-file edit} and
@emph{Agent} (below)
need the whole reply before they can act.  Whichever action is waiting on the
model, the @samp{AI} pane shows a rotating @samp{-\|/} indicator and the
seconds elapsed on its waiting line -- a slow local model looks busy, not
frozen -- and @key{Esc} aborts the wait at any time.  @key{Esc} leaves the
pane armed: the pane says @samp{[AI] cancelled}, its input line is ready for a
follow-up, and a second @key{Esc}
returns to the file window.  Edit, Complete and Multi-file edit work on a file
window only: asked from the pane itself, from Messages or from a read-only
viewer they send nothing and the pane says @samp{[AI] no file here - Edit,
Complete and Multi-file work on a file window}.

The editor stays live while a request is out: the menus answer, the cursor
moves, and your other windows are editable as always.  The @emph{text} of the
window that is waiting is locked, and its status row says
@samp{The assistant is working on this window (Esc cancels)}.  The reason is
that the reply is written back over the lines that were sent, so a keystroke
accepted meanwhile would come back to you as a change to revert, or would move
the lines the reply belongs to.  Asking for a second action while one runs is
not refused either: it is @dfn{queued}, the pane says which action is waiting,
and it starts by itself when the running one ends, on its own window.

@section Edit

@cindex AI, edit
@kbd{Alt-G e} asks for an instruction (``add error handling'', ``fix the compile
error'') and sends exactly one of these scopes: the marked block when one is
active, the whole file when it fits the context limit, or one consecutive window
of lines around the cursor when the file is larger.  For that window case the
pane says @samp{[AI edit] lines 431-770 of 1200 sent}, and the request carries a
header with the same line range.  The reply replaces exactly the text that was
sent, so every line outside that scope stays untouched.

A reply that could not be the new file is questioned before any diff opens: when
it is prose (``I cannot do that''), whitespace only, an empty pair of code fences
or so much shorter than the file that it reads as a truncation, xwpe asks in a
message box that names both line counts, @samp{reply has 1 line, file has 3:
review anyway?}.  @kbd{Yes} goes on to the review as usual; @kbd{No} or
@key{Esc} ends the edit there, the pane reports @samp{[AI edit] reply declined
(nothing changed)}, and buffer and file are untouched.

The reply need not be bare code.  When the model wraps the file in markdown
fences (a @code{```c} line before it and a @code{```} line after it), only the
content of the outermost pair of fences becomes the file, with the language tag
dropped and the text ending in exactly one newline.  Whatever the model wrote
around the fences -- a preamble such as ``Here is the updated file:'', a closing
remark, or a second fenced block holding a test -- is not file content: it is
shown in the assistant pane under the line @samp{[AI edit] the model's
commentary (not part of the file):}, so a note about the change is read and
never lands in the source.  A reply with no fence at all is taken whole, as the
file.  The question above about a too-short reply is asked about the extracted
code, so a long preamble cannot disguise a one-line refusal.

A reply with CRLF line endings is read as LF before anything is compared, so
only the lines the model really changed are shown and no carriage return reaches
the buffer; a file that is itself CRLF is the editor's business as always
(carriage returns stripped on load and written back on save).

The model's rewrite is shown as a @dfn{diff} you review hunk by hunk in a popup
inside the file window.  Before the popup opens, the file scrolls so the hunk's
first line is on screen and the cursor is placed on it, keeping its column, the
way Search and Goto Line bring a line into view.  The popup then sits one row
below that line (above it when there is no room below), never on the window's
status row and never over the assistant pane.  In a short window, where the
popup fits whole on neither side, it takes the side with more room and
@key{PgDn} shows the rest.  A row wider than the popup is cut at its edge and
ends in @samp{>}, so a change beyond the edge is never shown as two rows that
read the same.  @kbd{y} or @key{Enter} applies this hunk and @kbd{n} skips it.
@kbd{a} applies all remaining hunks, except that it stops at a hunk which
deletes more lines than it adds and shows that one for its own decision -- its
key line names the tally, @samp{deletes 2 adds 0} -- so no text is removed
unseen.  @key{PgUp} and @key{PgDn} scroll inside a hunk taller than the popup.

@kbd{q} or @key{Esc} cancels the review and leaves the file exactly as it was
before that review started: the hunks already accepted are dropped with the
rest, and the pane says how many, @samp{[AI edit] discarded (2 accepted hunks
dropped)}.  To stop reviewing and @emph{keep} what you have accepted, press
@kbd{n} on the hunks that are left.  The close glyph on the popup's title row
cancels the same way.  After @key{Esc} the popup is gone and the keyboard is the
editor's again: a letter typed next is inserted into the file as ordinary text,
it is not read as a review key.  Resizing the terminal while the popup is open
lays it out again for the new size, next to the same hunk, and the keys keep
working.

Applying uses one whole-buffer undo step, so @kbd{Ctrl-U} reverts the whole
edit.  When the review ends the cursor rests on the first line of the first hunk
you accepted, column kept, so that change is on screen.  Nothing is written to
disk until you @emph{save}.

@cindex AI, diagnostics
When a language server (@pxref{Language servers}) is running, the errors and
warnings it reports for the current file -- the ones you see underlined -- are
folded into the prompt for Ask, Edit and the agent, so ``fix this'' works without
pasting the message.

@section Multi-file edit

@cindex AI, multi-file edit
@cindex AI, workspace edit
@kbd{Alt-G f} tackles a change that spans several files (what some tools call a
@dfn{workspace edit}; it is @emph{not} an agent ``plan mode'' that only drafts
steps).  The assistant first @dfn{studies} the workspace -- the files you have
open, plus the members of the open project (@pxref{Projects}) and, if asked, the
surrounding folder -- reading whatever it needs with read-only tools.  It then
proposes the complete new content for each file it wants to change.
The action has one name wherever you meet it: the menu row, the prompt
(@samp{Multi-file edit: task}), the status lines in the assistant pane, which
are prefixed with @samp{[Multi-file edit]}, and the approval dialog's title.

xwpe collects the proposal without touching anything and shows it in a
@dfn{modal approval dialog} -- a boxed, colored popup listing each file with its
per-file line counts, so the choice is an explicit confirmation and nothing paints
under it:

@example
+------------- Multi-file edit - 4 files ----------------------------+
|   src/parser.c  (+12 -4)                                           |
|   src/parser.h  (+2 -0)                                            |
|   src/main.c    (+1 -1)                                            |
| ! ../shared/util.h  (+3 -0)  [new file]  outside the workspace     |
| Enter/A = Apply all   F = Review file-by-file   Q/Esc = Cancel     |
+--------------------------------------------------------------------+
@end example

The counts are taken against each file @emph{as the editor has it now}, unsaved
edits included, so they are the lines Apply all will add and remove; an edit you
typed before asking is counted, not lost.  A file that does not exist yet says
@samp{[new file]}.  A file the model wants to write outside the workspace -- the
project's directory when a project is open, else the directory of the file you
asked from -- carries a @samp{!} mark and the words @samp{outside the
workspace}, in the colour of a deletion.  It is not refused, the model may be
right that the file belongs there, but you see it before you approve, and the
pane names it again when it is applied.  This dialog, with the @kbd{f}
file-by-file review below, is the preview of a Multi-file change.

@key{Enter} or @kbd{a} applies every file; @kbd{f} opens each and lets you accept
it hunk by hunk (in the same diff box as Edit); @kbd{q} or @key{Esc} cancels.
Each changed file is @strong{opened in its own window and left open}, so you can
read it, step between them with @kbd{Alt-1}@dots{}, and undo any one with
@kbd{Ctrl-U}.

@section Agent and the permission dial

@cindex AI, agent
@cindex AI, permissions
@kbd{Alt-G g} runs the model as an autonomous agent with a small, bounded set of
tools: @code{read_file}, @code{list_dir}, @code{grep} and @code{glob} (find files
by name) run on their own, while @code{web_search}, @code{web_fetch},
@code{write_file}, @code{apply_patch} and @code{run_command} are governed by the
@dfn{permission dial}.  @code{web_fetch} reads a web page over the built-in TLS
client and @code{web_search} looks one up (its query engine is set by
@code{AISearchURL}), so a backend with no web tool of its own can still use
external information; both are network access, so they are gated like a command:

@table @asis
@item @code{ask} (default)
every write or command asks you @kbd{y}/@kbd{n}.
@item @code{edits}
writes go through automatically; commands still ask.
@item @code{auto}
everything runs unattended; you review the result afterwards.
@end table

Set the dial from the @samp{AI} menu, with @kbd{Alt-G y}, or with @code{AIPolicy}
/ @env{XWPE_AI_POLICY}.  @code{edits} and @code{auto} always take a checkpoint
first.  The dial is read live: raised with @kbd{Alt-G y} while a run waits for
the model, it applies to the next tool, and the first write that follows takes
the checkpoint the launch would have taken, with the same @samp{[agent]
checkpoint taken} line, so the review at the end covers a run that started
under @code{ask}.  With the @code{claudecli} backend the built-in agent runs the
CLI text-only: the model answers with the editor's own tool protocol and the
CLI's tools stay off, so the dial governs the editor's tools exactly as with any
other backend and the CLI's own @option{--permission-mode} is not involved (the
hosted engine, below, is where the dial maps onto it).  Under @code{auto} the
pane adds a tip pointing to that engine.

@cindex AI, tool argument
@cindex AI, outside the workspace
Whatever the dial, the argument of @code{list_dir}, @code{grep} and @code{glob}
reaches the shell as a single word: a pattern with spaces is one pattern, and no
character in it (a @samp{;}, a @samp{$(...)}) is a command, so a line planted in
a fetched page cannot run anything through a read-only tool.  A write to a file
outside the working tree (an absolute path beyond it, or one that climbs out
with @file{..}) always asks, under @code{edits} and @code{auto} too: the write
box carries the @samp{!} mark and the words @samp{outside the workspace}, as the
Multi-file dialog does, and the pane names the file again once it is written.

@cindex AI, command output
Whatever the dial allows, a command the assistant runs cannot reach your screen.
Each one runs in a session of its own with no terminal attached, so opening
@file{/dev/tty} fails and the only thing that comes back is the text xwpe reads
and shows in the @samp{AI} pane.  Without that, a command that wrote to
@file{/dev/tty} painted straight over the editor, around the screen library: the
display went black and the command's lines scrolled across the file until the
next repaint.  The same isolation applies to the hosted external agent CLI
described below and to the post-edit hook (@code{AIEditHook}).

@cindex AI, tool time limit
@cindex AIToolTimeout
While a command or a web tool runs, xwpe reads no keys, so the pane says what
is happening: its last line reads @samp{[agent] running run_command @dots{}
(result follows)} (or @code{web_search}, @code{web_fetch}) from before the run
starts until its result lands.  A command is given @code{AIToolTimeout} seconds
(default 60; also @env{XWPE_AI_TOOL_TIMEOUT}): when it runs longer, xwpe ends it
(the whole process group, TERM then KILL), the pane says @samp{run_command timed
out after @var{N} s} and the model reads @samp{TOOL ERROR: timed out after
@var{N} s}, so it can split the work or you can raise the key for a command that
legitimately needs longer.  The bound reaches the command's process group; a
command that detaches itself from it (@command{setsid}, @command{nohup @dots{}
&}) is beyond it and runs on.  The read-only tools and the post-edit hook run
through the same function and share the limit.  The web tools have their own,
shorter time limit on the connection.  A tool result is bounded in size as well:
a command's output or a file read with @code{read_file} is cut at 6000 bytes and
then ends with the line @samp{(output cut at 6000 bytes)}, so the model knows it
saw the beginning only and asks for a narrower command or a grep instead of
taking the first part for the whole.

When you answer a request with @kbd{n} or @key{Esc}, the pane records it:
@samp{[agent] denied: run_command @var{cmd}}, and likewise for
@code{web_search}, @code{web_fetch}, @code{write_file} and @code{apply_patch}.
The model is told @samp{(denied by user)} and the run goes on; the trace
(@env{XWPE_AI_TRACE}) carries the same line, so a denial is never a request that
seemed to vanish.  A write that cannot be made (its directory does not exist) is
told the same way: the pane says @samp{[agent] write failed: @var{path}}, nothing
is created, the model reads @samp{(write failed)} and the run goes on.

@cindex AI, write_file body
The body of a @code{write_file} is the file, written verbatim: every line with
its newline, the last one included, so a text file the agent writes ends with a
newline as one saved by the editor does, and CRLF content lands as it was sent.
@code{apply_patch} edits that text in place and keeps the same ending.

When an agent task is really a question rather than a change, the model answers
directly: it finishes with a short line and its answer follows in the pane, so you
see the reply even though this is not the chat.  A reply with neither a
@samp{TOOL} line nor that closing line is taken as the answer as well: it is
shown whole, the run ends and the follow-up prompt opens under it.

@cindex AI, transport error
@cindex AI, backend error
A turn that fails is reported as a failure, never as a finished task.  When the
server answers an error, the pane says @samp{[agent] working: backend error:
@var{message}} with the server's own words (@samp{model m is on fire}, @samp{The
model `x' does not exist}); the run stops, no tool runs, the session is saved and
the review opens if files were written.  When the connection itself dies (closed
with no bytes, an answer that is not HTTP, a reply cut before its end) the pane
says @samp{[AI] transport error}: the run stops, nothing is applied, and neither
@samp{[agent] done} nor the follow-up prompt appears, so a dead server cannot be
mistaken for a completed task.  Chat, Edit and Complete report the same failure
under their own prefix (@samp{[AI edit] transport error}, and so on).

@cindex AI, apply_patch
@cindex AI, plan mode
To change an existing file the agent prefers @code{apply_patch} -- a precise
@code{SEARCH}/@code{REPLACE} edit rather than a whole-file rewrite -- which goes
through the same review, checkpoint and one-step @kbd{Ctrl-U} undo as
@code{write_file}, and a search that does not match is reported back for a retry
instead of a bad write.  For a multi-step task the agent can first lay out a
@dfn{plan}: it loads a @samp{[ ] task} checklist into the @samp{Messages} window
@emph{before} it acts, so you see the intended steps, and redraws it in place --
@samp{3/7 done}, @samp{[x]} per finished task -- as it reports each step, a live
to-do list you can bring up alongside the run; compact progress also shows in the
@samp{AI} pane so you see it advance without switching windows.

@section Build & fix

@cindex AI, build and fix
@kbd{Alt-G b}, @samp{Build & fix (agent)}, is a canned agent for the commonest
loop.  It picks the build
command -- @command{make} when a makefile is present, otherwise a syntax-only
compile of the current file by its language -- shows it, and if the build fails
hands the agent a loop: run the build, read the compiler errors, edit the
sources, build again, repeat until it passes.  It uses the same tools and
permission dial as @kbd{Alt-G g}, so you approve edits and commands exactly as in
a normal agent run.

@section Complete at cursor

@cindex AI, completion
@kbd{Alt-G c}, @samp{Complete at cursor}, fills in code where the cursor is: xwpe
sends the nearby context to the model (the last 2000 characters before the cursor
and the first 800 after it).  When the reply arrives it is not written into the
file yet: a one-hunk box titled @samp{Proposed completion} shows the suggestion
at its place, in the same colours and with the same keys as the Edit review, and
the file changes only when you take it with @kbd{y} or @key{Enter}; @kbd{n},
@kbd{q} or @key{Esc} leave the file untouched and the pane says
@samp{[AI complete] discarded}.  A reply that arrives while a dialog is open
waits, like an Edit's, and opens its box once the dialog closes.

An accepted suggestion is inserted exactly as the model returned it, cleaned only
of markdown fences: no line break is added after it, so completing @code{foo(}
with @code{bar)} reads @code{foo(bar)} on one line.  It goes in as one
whole-buffer undo step, the pane says @samp{completed at cursor - Ctrl-U to undo},
and one @kbd{Ctrl-U} removes it.  It is a local, low-ceremony alternative to Chat
or Edit for ``finish this line/block''; the editor stays interactive while the
model works.

@section Hosting an external agent (Claude Code)

@cindex AI, agent host
@cindex Claude Code
Everything above is @emph{agnostic}: xwpe drives the model itself.  A further,
separately compiled option (@option{--enable-ai-agent-host}, off by default) lets
the @strong{Agent} run on a different @dfn{engine}: instead of the editor's own
tool loop, it hosts a real coding-agent CLI -- @strong{Claude Code} first -- that
runs @emph{its own} tools, with xwpe as the front-end.

Choose the engine in @samp{Options}, @samp{AI} (the @samp{Agent engine} radio:
@samp{Built-in} or @samp{Claude Code}).  With @samp{Claude Code} selected,
@kbd{Alt-G g} starts a persistent @command{claude} session (a bidirectional
stream-json subprocess on the fd-loop).  Its answer text and tool activity stream
into the pane; when one of its tools writes a file, xwpe reloads that file into
its open window as one undo step, so @kbd{Ctrl-U} reverts the agent's edit.  The
permission dial gates the agent's tools @emph{per tool}, with the CLI's tools
classed like the editor's own: @code{ask} pops a @kbd{y}/@kbd{n} for each gated
tool call (the CLI blocks until you answer); @code{edits} auto-accepts the
file-editing tools (@code{Write}, @code{Edit}, @code{MultiEdit},
@code{NotebookEdit}) with the line @samp{[agent] auto-approved (edits): ...} and
asks with the command box for @code{Bash}, @code{WebFetch}, @code{WebSearch} and
any tool it does not know; @code{auto} runs unattended.  A refusal answers the
CLI ``Denied by the user'' and the pane shows @samp{[agent] denied: <tool>
<arg>}, as for a built-in tool.  Either way xwpe takes a checkpoint at the start
of the session and says so with the same @samp{[agent] checkpoint taken} line as
the built-in loop, so the whole run is reviewable/revertible when it ends.  The
session stays live across turns: @kbd{Alt-G g} feeds the next turn, and an empty
prompt (or @key{Esc} at that prompt) ends it and offers the changeset review.

@key{Esc} while a turn is streaming interrupts that turn, as it cancels a
built-in run: the pane says @samp{[agent] interrupting ...}, the editor sends the
engine its interrupt request on the session's channel, and when the engine ends
the turn the pane shows @samp{[agent] turn interrupted - Alt-G g continues, Esc
there ends} with the session still alive.  An engine that has not ended the turn
within @code{AIToolTimeout} seconds (default 60) is ended as if it had exited:
the review opens for anything written and the pane says @samp{[agent] <engine>
session ended}.

It is built as a generic host, so which CLI it runs, and how its output is read,
are configurable (@code{AIHostCommand}, @code{AIHostAdapter}).  Two adapters
exist: @code{claude-code} (the stream-json event protocol above) and @code{text}
-- a plain prompt-in/text-out CLI whose stdout is rendered as the answer, for
hosting @command{aider}, @command{sgpt} or any script (one turn per
invocation, without the tool/edit/permission integration the stream-json protocol
gives).  Further agent CLIs are new adapters, not a fork of this control flow.  A
build without @option{--enable-ai-agent-host} contains none of it, and the
@samp{Agent engine} radio does not appear.

@section Reviewing changes like compile errors

@cindex AI, review
@cindex AI, checkpoint
@cindex AI, revert
Before an @code{edits} or @code{auto} run, xwpe takes a @dfn{checkpoint}: a
@command{git} reference when the workspace is a git tree, otherwise a private
snapshot of the files under @file{$XDG_STATE_HOME/xwpe/checkpoints/} (default
@file{~/.local/state/xwpe/checkpoints/}).  Afterwards it writes
the @dfn{changeset} into the @samp{Messages} window, one line per changed hunk in
the compiler's @code{file:line:} format -- so you step through the AI's changes
with the very same @kbd{Alt-T} (Next) and @kbd{Alt-V} (Previous) keys you use for
compile errors, each jump opening the file at the change.

A review prompt then offers @kbd{a} keep all, @kbd{r} revert everything back to
the checkpoint, @kbd{f} decide file by file, and (in a git tree) @kbd{c} commit
the change as one auditable commit.  Reverting a file the agent created deletes
it.  This makes trying a weaker local model in @code{auto} safe: if it makes a
mess, one @kbd{r} puts you back.

@section Sessions

@cindex AI, sessions
The conversation is tied to the workspace and resumes automatically.  For
@code{claudecli} xwpe keeps the CLI's own session id and passes @option{--resume};
for the other backends it keeps a short transcript that seeds the next prompt.
It is stored beside the project file (as @file{.xwpe-ai-session}) when a project
is open, otherwise under @file{$XDG_STATE_HOME/xwpe/ai/} (default
@file{~/.local/state/xwpe/ai/}).  @kbd{Alt-G n}, @samp{Clear conversation},
forgets the current session and starts fresh.

@section Configuration

@cindex AI, configuration
These keys live in the @code{[Programming]} section of @file{xwperc}
(@pxref{Configuration}), now kept under @file{$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/xwpe/} (default
@file{~/.config/xwpe/}, migrated from the legacy @file{~/.xwpe/} on first run).
Changing the AI working mode -- the backend, model or permission level -- saves
itself immediately, so it sticks across sessions without @samp{Save Options}:

@cindex AI, project instructions
@cindex AGENTS.md
@cindex .xwpe-ai.md
If the working directory (the project root) holds a @file{.xwpe-ai.md} -- or,
when that file is absent, an @file{AGENTS.md} -- its contents are folded into
the prompt under a @samp{PROJECT INSTRUCTIONS} heading, so a repo's standing
rules (the build command, code style, ``do not touch @file{vendor/}'') are
followed without restating them.  The editor's own file wins, so a repository
whose @file{AGENTS.md} serves other coding agents can still give this editor
its own instructions.  Where the block goes is set by
@code{AIProjectInstructions}: under @code{agent}, the default, the Agent, the
Multi-file edit and Build & fix carry it, and the chat carries it from the turn
in which it first uses a tool, for the rest of the session; a plain Ask does
not, because the file exists for work that acts on the repository and on the
current file it is context spent for nothing, a tenth of a local model's
window.  @code{all} folds it into every chat turn as well; @code{off} never
folds it.  Edit and Complete never carry it: their prompts ask for file text
only.  The pane says so: an agent run shows @samp{[agent] project instructions:
.xwpe-ai.md (@var{N} bytes)} right after its task line, and the chat shows the
same line once per session, on the first turn that carries the block.  The
prompt carries at most @code{AIProjectInstructionsMax} bytes of the file
(default 4095) so it cannot crowd out the prompt; a longer file is cut and the
line says so, @samp{(@var{N} bytes, cut at @var{M}; AIProjectInstructionsMax
raises it)}, so a rule near its end that the model never saw is not a mystery
and the key that shows all of it is on the same line.
Project instructions govern how the tools are used, never which tools exist:
the prompt states, right after the block, that the tool list above stands, so a
rule written for other coding agents (``do not open network connections'') does
not make the model refuse the editor's own @code{web_search}.

@table @code
@item AIBackend
@code{ollama} (0), @code{openai} (1), @code{claude} (2), @code{claudecli} (4).
@item AIEndpoint
Base URL for the HTTP backends (default @code{http://localhost:11434}); also
editable in the @samp{Endpoint URL} field of @samp{Options}, @samp{AI}.
@item AIModel
Model name; empty means ``pick the first the server offers''.
@item AICAFile
Path to an extra CA or self-signed certificate to trust for HTTPS, for a local
bridge that serves TLS with its own certificate (e.g. a Proton Lumo bridge on
@code{https://127.0.0.1:8443}).  Editable in the @samp{CA file} field of
@samp{Options}, @samp{AI} (@key{Alt-C}).  It is trusted @emph{in addition to} the
system CA store and verification still runs in full (chain and hostname) -- it is
not a blanket ``skip verify''.  Empty means the system CA store only.
@item AISearchURL
URL template for the agent's @code{web_search} tool, with @code{%s} where the
URL-encoded query goes (e.g. @code{https://searx.example/search?q=%s&format=json}
for a SearXNG instance).  Also settable with @env{XWPE_AI_SEARCH_URL}.  Empty
means a built-in keyless default.
@item AIToolTimeout
Seconds a command run for a tool result may take before xwpe ends it and tells
the model @samp{TOOL ERROR: timed out after @var{N} s}; default 60.  Also
settable with @env{XWPE_AI_TOOL_TIMEOUT}, for one session.  Covers the agent's
@code{run_command}, the read-only tools (@code{list_dir}, @code{grep},
@code{glob}) in every loop, and the post-edit hook (@code{AIEditHook}).  The
bound ends the command's process group; a command that detaches itself from it
(@command{setsid}, @command{nohup @dots{} &}) is beyond it.  A value that is not
a positive number means the default; the key is written to @file{xwperc} only
when it differs from the default.
@item AIProjectInstructions
Where the project instructions file (@file{.xwpe-ai.md}, else @file{AGENTS.md})
is folded into the prompt: @code{agent} (the default: the Agent, the Multi-file
edit, Build & fix, and the chat from the turn in which it first uses a tool),
@code{all} (every chat turn as well) or @code{off} (never).  Any other value
reads as @code{agent}.  Also settable with @env{XWPE_AI_PROJECT_INSTRUCTIONS},
for one session; written to @file{xwperc} only when it differs from the default.
@item AIProjectInstructionsMax
Bytes of the project instructions file the prompt carries; default 4095, kept
small so the file cannot crowd a local model's window.  A longer file is cut
and the pane line names this key.  A value above 65536 is capped there; a
value that is not a positive number means the default.  Also settable with
@env{XWPE_AI_PROJECT_INSTRUCTIONS_MAX}, for one session; written to
@file{xwperc} only when it differs from the default.
@item AIProvider
A saved OpenAI-compatible provider profile, as
@code{name|endpoint|model|cafile} (model and cafile optional); repeat the key for
several.  Pick one, or save the current settings as a new one, with the
@samp{Provider} button (@key{Alt-V}) in @samp{Options}, @samp{AI}: it switches the
backend to OpenAI-compatible and applies the profile's endpoint, model and CA
file.  Each profile's API key lives in
@file{~/.config/xwpe/openai-api-key-@var{name}} (falling back to the generic
@file{openai-api-key}), so Groq, a Proton Lumo bridge and so on can each carry
their own key.
@item AIProviderName
The active profile's name (empty for an ad-hoc endpoint typed into the field).
@item AIPolicy
@code{ask}, @code{edits} or @code{auto}.
@item AIModelFallback
A model retried once, automatically, when the primary fails a turn (unavailable,
or too slow / timed out); xwpe says @samp{primary model failed @dots{} retrying
with @var{fallback}} and continues on it.  Empty disables the fallback.  Editable
in @samp{Options}, @samp{AI}, @samp{Advanced@dots{}} (@key{Alt-A}), with the two
below.
@item AIEditHook
A command run on a file the assistant wrote, patched or edited, e.g.@:
@code{clang-format -i}, so AI edits stay consistent with the repo's tooling
(like format-on-save).  The path is appended, quoted, so a name with a space
in it reaches the command whole.  After an agent @code{write_file} or
@code{apply_patch} the hook runs on the file on disk and the open window takes
its result inside the undo step the write took: one @kbd{Ctrl-U} puts back the
text from before the write, in the buffer only, since the disk keeps the agent's
text until you save or revert it in the changeset review, and the pane says
@samp{Ctrl-U to undo (buffer only)}.  After an accepted Edit, completion or
multi-file change the hook runs on a temporary copy of the buffer beside the
file (same directory and extension, so a formatter finds its configuration),
the copy's text replaces the buffer within that same single undo step, and
nothing is written to the file until you save.  A hook that exits non-zero is
reported in the pane with its exit status and its output, and the buffer is
left as the assistant left it.  The hook runs through the same function as a
tool command and inherits its time limit (@code{AIToolTimeout}): a hook that
hangs is ended after that many seconds and reported as failed, with the timeout
as its output, instead of holding the editor.
@item AIAgentEngine
@code{builtin} (the editor's own tool loop) or @code{claude-code} (host the
external agent for @kbd{Alt-G g}; see @emph{Hosting an external agent}, above);
the latter needs a build configured with @option{--enable-ai-agent-host}.
@item AIHostCommand
The agent CLI to host when @code{AIAgentEngine} is @code{claude-code}; empty runs
@command{claude}.  A different command (@command{aider}, @command{opencode},
@dots{}) is run through the shell, so hosting another agent needs no build change.
Overridden by @env{XWPE_AI_HOST_CMD}.
@item AIHostAdapter
How the hosted CLI's output is read: @code{claude-code} (the default -- the
stream-json event protocol, with tool calls, on-disk edits reflected into your
buffers, and per-tool permission prompts) or @code{text} (a plain
prompt-in/text-out CLI: xwpe writes the prompt to its stdin, closes it, and
renders the CLI's stdout as the answer; one turn per invocation, no tool or edit
reflection).  Use @code{text} to host @command{aider --message}, @command{sgpt}
or any prompt-to-text script.  Overridden by @env{XWPE_AI_HOST_ADAPTER}.
@end table

Environment overrides (handy for scripts and tests): @env{XWPE_AI_ENABLE},
@env{XWPE_AI_BACKEND}, @env{XWPE_AI_ENDPOINT}, @env{XWPE_AI_MODEL},
@env{XWPE_AI_CAFILE}, @env{XWPE_AI_POLICY}, @env{XWPE_AI_AGENT_ENGINE}
(@code{builtin}/@code{claude-code}), @env{XWPE_AI_HOST_CMD} (the agent CLI to
host), @env{XWPE_AI_HOST_ADAPTER} (@code{claude-code}/@code{text}), and
@env{XWPE_AI_TRACE} (a path to log assistant events to).  API keys are read from the standard environment variable first
(@env{ANTHROPIC_API_KEY}, @env{OPENAI_API_KEY}), then from @code{@var{NAME}_FILE},
then from @file{~/.config/xwpe/anthropic-api-key} (or @file{openai-api-key}, or a
provider's own @file{openai-api-key-@var{name}}); the @code{claudecli} backend
needs none of this.  An OpenAI key can also be typed into the @samp{API key} field
of @samp{Options}, @samp{AI} (@key{Alt-K}): it is used at once (so @key{Alt-M}
lists an authenticated endpoint's models) and written to the active provider's
key file.  The field shows the key stored for the current provider, so you can
see one is set (it comes from the key file, not from any @env{OPENAI_API_KEY}
override).
